812
01:04:00,370 --> 01:04:03,940
So I have a sequence.

813
01:04:03,940 --> 01:04:10,580
And so I have a sequence
and then a subsequence.

814
01:04:10,580 --> 01:04:12,560
So I'll give you
first some examples.

815
01:04:12,560 --> 01:04:15,030
And then I will explain.

816
01:04:15,030 --> 01:04:17,520
Then I will give you
the proper definition

817
01:04:17,520 --> 01:04:21,880
of what a subsequence is.

818
01:04:21,880 --> 01:04:28,870
So I'll start with this
sequence from before.

819
01:04:28,870 --> 01:04:33,690
So this was the sequence where
a1 was minus 1, a2 was 1,

820
01:04:33,690 --> 01:04:35,740
a3 was minus 1.

821
01:04:35,740 --> 01:04:40,770
It was alternating
between minus 1 and 1.

822
01:04:40,770 --> 01:04:43,770
And so, in this case here--

823
01:04:43,770 --> 01:04:49,950
in this case here, if
you take the sequence--

824
01:04:49,950 --> 01:04:57,180
let's call it-- let's call it b.

825
01:04:57,180 --> 01:05:01,030
Let's call it-- let's
use a different index.

826
01:05:01,030 --> 01:05:01,985
Let's call it bk.

827
01:05:04,590 --> 01:05:07,030
So k here plays the
same role as n before.

828
01:05:07,030 --> 01:05:10,440
But I want to use a different
symbol to not somehow confuse

829
01:05:10,440 --> 01:05:13,060
with the n over here.

830
01:05:13,060 --> 01:05:15,230
So if I take--

831
01:05:15,230 --> 01:05:19,720
so I could take a sequence
that was like 1 all the time,

832
01:05:19,720 --> 01:05:20,990
this here, for all k.

833
01:05:23,920 --> 01:05:26,390
This here would
