844
01:06:09,160 --> 01:06:11,440
So you could have
a sequence that was

845
01:06:11,440 --> 01:06:14,980
like b1 is equal to minus 1.

846
01:06:14,980 --> 01:06:19,440
b2 is equal to minus 1.

847
01:06:19,440 --> 01:06:25,540
b3-- now it becomes
the next two even ones.

848
01:06:25,540 --> 01:06:29,650
And then you go back to the
next two odd ones, et cetera.

849
01:06:29,650 --> 01:06:32,656
That would be
another subsequence.

850
01:06:36,840 --> 01:06:38,863
Let me look at a
few more examples.

851
01:06:49,350 --> 01:06:53,760
So, for instance,
if your sequence,

852
01:06:53,760 --> 01:06:55,920
your original sequence--

853
01:06:55,920 --> 01:06:58,980
so here's another example.

854
01:06:58,980 --> 01:07:07,470
If your original sequence was
just that a n was equal to n,

855
01:07:07,470 --> 01:07:10,820
then you could take a sequence.

856
01:07:10,820 --> 01:07:17,110
So then the sequence where
you just take the odd numbers,

857
01:07:17,110 --> 01:07:19,220
this here is a subsequence.

858
01:07:23,200 --> 01:07:28,840
And likewise, if you take
just the even numbers,

859
01:07:28,840 --> 01:07:33,500
this is also a
sequence, a subsequence.

860
01:07:38,160 --> 01:07:40,270
But if you're looking at--

861
01:07:40,270 --> 01:07:45,470
if you look at, say, something
like this, where you're taking--

862
01:07:45,470 --> 01:07:50,834
so if you define a sequence
where it was 1, 1--

863
01:07:53,770 --> 01:07:56,523
1, 1, 2, even like this--

864
01:08:00,850 --> 01:08:02,990
this here is not a subsequence.

865
01:08:02,990 --> 01:08:12,030
This is not a subsequence,
because you have--

866
01:08:12,030 --> 01:08:15,930
what you have done here is that
you have taken the same number

867
01:08:15,930 --> 01:08:17,140
several times.

868
01:08:17,140 --> 01:08:18,880
And it doesn't
appear several times.

869
01:08:18,880 --> 01:08:22,080
The value doesn't appear several
times in the original one.

870
01:08:22,080 --> 01:08:24,450
So that's not allowed.

871
01:08:24,450 --> 01:08:28,930
It's also not allowed to
do something like this.

872
01:08:28,930 --> 01:08:32,470
This here is also not
a-- so not a subsequence,

873
01:08:32,470 --> 01:08:35,490
another thing that's
not a subsequence--

874
01:08:35,490 --> 01:08:37,750
when you take
things out of order.

875
01:08:37,750 --> 01:08:42,640
So if this here was
the original sequence,

876
01:08:42,640 --> 01:08:48,540
if you're now looking at
a sequence that was 3, 2,

877
01:08:48,540 --> 01:08:52,710
and then 4, 5, this
here is-- et cetera--

878
01:08:52,710 --> 01:08:55,710
this here is not a
subsequence either,

879
01:08:55,710 --> 01:08:59,819
because things are
taken out of order.

880
01:08:59,819 --> 01:09:03,346
So now, what is the formal
definition of a subsequence?

881
01:09:05,880 --> 01:09:07,906
So here is a formal definition.

882
01:09:30,100 --> 01:09:33,669
So you have a sequence, a n.

883
01:09:33,669 --> 01:09:49,210
A subsequence of a n is where
you have a map, g, from N

884
01:09:49,210 --> 01:09:53,890
into N. This is
an increasing map.

885
01:09:53,890 --> 01:09:55,520
This is strictly increasing.

886
01:10:02,920 --> 01:10:06,780
And then, if you're thinking
about this thing here,

887
01:10:06,780 --> 01:10:10,950
you think about this
as a function, f,

888
01:10:10,950 --> 01:10:15,450
then you now could think about
the composition of these two

889
01:10:15,450 --> 01:10:19,380
functions, so this thing here.

890
01:10:19,380 --> 01:10:22,950
And maybe I will use
a different notation

891
01:10:22,950 --> 01:10:27,070
like this, a different
index like this.

892
01:10:27,070 --> 01:10:34,170
This thing here, bk, this
here is a subsequence.

893
01:10:34,170 --> 01:10:38,470
So you see that this
here, when you pick--

894
01:10:38,470 --> 01:10:42,870
if you have a
sequence like this,

895
01:10:42,870 --> 01:10:46,750
the first one could be
anything in the original one.

896
01:10:46,750 --> 01:10:50,400
But once you have fixed the
first one, you can't look back,

897
01:10:50,400 --> 01:10:52,170
or you can't go to this.

898
01:10:52,170 --> 01:10:55,740
You have to-- the next element
have to be somewhat further

899
01:10:55,740 --> 01:10:57,427
out in the sequence.

900
01:10:57,427 --> 01:11:01,682
And that continues.

901
01:11:01,682 --> 01:11:05,660
And this here is often
just to indicate that it's

902
01:11:05,660 --> 01:11:07,980
a subsequence of this sequence.

903
01:11:07,980 --> 01:11:15,440
Then the standard notation is
a n and then another subscript.

904
01:11:15,440 --> 01:11:17,930
So anyway, so of
course, hopefully,

905
01:11:17,930 --> 01:11:20,480
there's not too many
subsequences of sequences,

906
01:11:20,480 --> 01:11:25,730
because each time, it's supposed
to be another subscript.

907
01:11:25,730 --> 01:11:28,250
Now, there's a little
theorem that we can just

908
01:11:28,250 --> 01:11:30,140
prove here at the end.

909
01:11:30,140 --> 01:11:34,130
And that is that
if you-- and it's--

910
01:11:34,130 --> 01:11:38,940
yeah, it's sometimes--
it's a very simple fact.

911
01:11:38,940 --> 01:11:43,280
But it is sometimes
a useful fact.

912
01:11:43,280 --> 01:11:48,300
And that is that if
you take a sequence--

913
01:11:48,300 --> 01:11:58,280
so a sequence--
so theorem, that--

914
01:11:58,280 --> 01:12:11,340
so a sequence, a n, is
convergent to a if and only

915
01:12:11,340 --> 01:12:37,380
if all subsequences
of a n converge to a.

916
01:12:37,380 --> 01:12:38,140
OK.

917
01:12:38,140 --> 01:12:43,500
Now, to prove this, there's
two things we need to prove,

918
01:12:43,500 --> 01:12:46,720
because it's an "if
and only if" statement.

919
01:12:46,720 --> 01:12:49,930
So we need to prove that
if it is convergent,

920
01:12:49,930 --> 01:12:52,870
then all subsequences
are also convergent.

921
01:12:52,870 --> 01:12:55,890
And then we need to prove
that if all subsequences are

922
01:12:55,890 --> 01:12:59,080
convergent, then the original
sequence is convergent.

923
01:12:59,080 --> 01:13:01,800
But one of the two
implications is trivial.

924
01:13:01,800 --> 01:13:13,960
So one of the two
implications is trivial.

925
01:13:16,880 --> 01:13:25,940
And that is that,
namely, since--

926
01:13:25,940 --> 01:13:30,920
so the map-- remember that the
map to define a subsequence

927
01:13:30,920 --> 01:13:34,620
has to be strictly increasing.

928
01:13:34,620 --> 01:13:42,470
So remember that a sequence
was a function like this.

929
01:13:42,470 --> 01:13:44,280
That was a sequence.

930
01:13:44,280 --> 01:13:47,600
And a subsequence-- there,
you have another map

931
01:13:47,600 --> 01:13:50,600
that is strictly increasing.

932
01:13:50,600 --> 01:13:53,100
And then you're looking
at the composition.

933
01:13:53,100 --> 01:13:57,440
But if you take this other
map to be the identity,

934
01:13:57,440 --> 01:14:03,250
that the other map is like g
of k is equal to k, of course,

935
01:14:03,250 --> 01:14:05,130
this is strictly increasing.

936
01:14:05,130 --> 01:14:09,810
And if you did-- if this here
map was the identity, then

937
01:14:09,810 --> 01:14:14,004
the subsequence would just
be the original sequence.

938
01:14:14,004 --> 01:14:16,980
So one of the two
implications here

939
01:14:16,980 --> 01:14:27,400
is trivial, since a n itself
is admittedly trivial.

940
01:14:27,400 --> 01:14:28,900
But it is a subsequence.

941
01:14:35,860 --> 01:14:41,280
So if all subsequences
converge to a, well, then

942
01:14:41,280 --> 01:14:45,610
the original sequence,
since it is a subsequence,

943
01:14:45,610 --> 01:14:47,250
certainly, it will converge.

944
01:14:47,250 --> 01:14:49,050
So that was trivial.

945
01:14:49,050 --> 01:14:54,780
OK, so we just need to prove
that if a n converged to a, then

946
01:14:54,780 --> 01:14:57,230
any subsequence.

947
01:14:57,230 --> 01:15:02,900
But this is not much more
difficult, because you see,

948
01:15:02,900 --> 01:15:07,310
if you're-- so
given an epsilon--

949
01:15:07,310 --> 01:15:08,945
so now, given epsilon--

950
01:15:12,260 --> 01:15:21,335
so now we have-- so we now
have that a n converges to a.

951
01:15:21,335 --> 01:15:24,960
And we're looking at
a subsequence, ank.

952
01:15:24,960 --> 01:15:35,180
And we want to show that
ank also converges to a.

953
01:15:35,180 --> 01:15:41,540
So given epsilon, given
epsilon greater than 0,

954
01:15:41,540 --> 01:15:51,590
there exists a capital N so
that if little n is bigger

955
01:15:51,590 --> 01:15:57,960
than capital N, then a n
minus a is less than epsilon.

956
01:15:57,960 --> 01:16:06,030
But now you see that if k
is bigger than capital N,

957
01:16:06,030 --> 01:16:10,740
then what corresponds to--

958
01:16:10,740 --> 01:16:17,730
this here is-- this here, how to
think about it-- this here, nk,

959
01:16:17,730 --> 01:16:20,520
you should really think
about that this thing here

960
01:16:20,520 --> 01:16:24,742
is the value of this
strictly increasing function.

961
01:16:24,742 --> 01:16:26,700
But this thing here is--

962
01:16:26,700 --> 01:16:30,240
because it's strictly
increasing, it's bigger than k.

963
01:16:30,240 --> 01:16:35,080
So you see that if this thing
here is bigger than this,

964
01:16:35,080 --> 01:16:38,910
then means that this index
here is further out than,

965
01:16:38,910 --> 01:16:43,860
in particular, the
capital N. And so this

966
01:16:43,860 --> 01:16:47,880
means that if you have
this here, then ank,

967
01:16:47,880 --> 01:16:51,310
this is some element like this.

968
01:16:51,310 --> 01:16:54,810
But it's further
out than capital N.

969
01:16:54,810 --> 01:16:57,770
So this thing here
surely will also

970
01:16:57,770 --> 01:17:02,180
be less than epsilon because
it's falling under here.

971
01:17:02,180 --> 01:17:09,450
So that implication
wasn't too much harder.

972
01:17:09,450 --> 01:17:12,904
And that's sometimes
useful, that--

973
01:17:12,904 --> 01:17:19,370
so if-- yeah, it's sometimes
useful that you're looking

974
01:17:19,370 --> 01:17:28,238
at a sequence, and you see that
if it is converging, then--

975
01:17:28,238 --> 01:17:29,780
you're looking at
a sequence, and you

976
01:17:29,780 --> 01:17:32,670
see that there's a subsequence
that is not converging,

977
01:17:32,670 --> 01:17:36,140
or you have that there are
two different subsequences

978
01:17:36,140 --> 01:17:39,440
of a given sequence that
converge to different limits.

979
01:17:39,440 --> 01:17:42,780
Then the original sequence
wasn't converging either.

980
01:17:42,780 --> 01:17:43,850
Yeah?

981
01:17:43,850 --> 01:17:46,310
AUDIENCE: Can you not
have a finite subsequence

982
01:17:46,310 --> 01:17:49,910
of-- a convergent subsequence
that doesn't get to n,

983
01:17:49,910 --> 01:17:54,700
so it hasn't arrived at
the point of convergence?

984
01:17:54,700 --> 01:17:56,460
TOBIAS COLDING: So
convergence is always

985
01:17:56,460 --> 01:18:00,310
something about the tail,
about the end, right?

986
01:18:00,310 --> 01:18:05,282
It never cares about what
happened in the beginning.

987
01:18:05,282 --> 01:18:08,070
So there might be
